(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-11-09"] = {"phy-11-09-m01":{"idea":"Electric charge has its own SI unit, named after Coulomb.","steps":["The SI unit of charge is the coulomb (C).","One coulomb is the charge carried past a point in one second by a current of one ampere.","So Q = I t."],"others":"The volt measures potential, the ampere measures current, and the farad measures capacitance.","trap":"Ampere is current. Coulomb is charge.","answer":"coulomb (C)"},"phy-11-09-m02":{"idea":"Quantisation means charge comes in whole-number packets of e.","steps":["The smallest free charge is e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C.","Any charge is q = n e, where n is a whole number.","So a body can have ±e, ±2e, ±3e, but never 1.5e."],"others":"A charge is not always equal to one electron's charge. It cannot take any value. It is also not tied to multiples of 1 coulomb.","trap":"Quarks carry fractions of e, but they are never found free.","answer":"is a whole-number multiple of the charge e of an electron, q = ne"},"phy-11-09-m03":{"idea":"Only electrons move between bodies. A positive charge means missing electrons.","steps":["Protons are fixed in the nuclei, so they do not move in charging.","A positive body has lost electrons.","Number lost n = q/e = 3.2 × 10⁻⁶/1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ = 2 × 10¹³."],"others":"Protons are not lost or gained in charging. Gaining electrons would make the body negative.","trap":"Positive charge is a shortage of electrons, not an extra of protons.","answer":"lost 2 × 10¹³ electrons"},"phy-11-09-m04":{"idea":"Charge can be moved around but the total in an isolated system cannot change.","steps":["Charge is moved from one body to another during charging.","The algebraic sum of all charges in an isolated system stays the same.","Even when pairs of opposite charges appear or vanish, the total stays the same."],"others":"The number of charged particles can change. Opposite charges meeting do not destroy charge, they cancel in the sum. Charge is not created from energy alone.","trap":"The conserved thing is the total (algebraic) charge, not the number of particles.","answer":"the total charge of an isolated system stays constant"},"phy-11-09-m05":{"idea":"Coulomb's force falls with the square of the distance and grows with the product of the charges.","steps":["The force is F = k q₁q₂/r².","It acts along the line joining the charges.","k = 1/(4πε₀) = 9 × 10⁹ N m²/C²."],"others":"Dividing by r (not r²) is the wrong power. Multiplying by r² would make the force grow with distance. Using the sum q₁ + q₂ ignores that force needs both charges.","trap":"Inverse square, along the line between the charges.","answer":"F = k q₁q₂/r², along the line joining them"},"phy-11-09-m06":{"idea":"For an inverse-square law, tripling the distance divides the force by nine.","steps":["F ∝ 1/r².","The new distance is 3r.","The new force is F/3² = F/9."],"others":"9 times and 3 times would grow the force with distance. 1/3 uses 1/r instead of 1/r².","trap":"Square the factor, then invert.","answer":"1/9 of the original"},"phy-11-09-m07":{"idea":"A medium reduces the electric force between charges by the factor K.","steps":["In a medium, ε₀ is replaced by ε = Kε₀.","So F = q₁q₂/(4πε r²) = F_vacuum/K.","The force is smaller by the factor K (about 80 for water)."],"others":"K² would be too big a reduction. The force is not unchanged. It is not larger, because K is greater than 1.","trap":"Force in a medium is F_vacuum divided by K.","answer":"smaller by the factor K"},"phy-11-09-m08":{"idea":"Electric forces add as vectors. Each charge acts as if the others were not there.","steps":["A third charge does not change the force between two charges.","So find the force from each charge alone.","Then add these forces as vectors."],"others":"A plain sum of magnitudes ignores direction. The nearest charge is not the only one that acts. The forces do not always cancel.","trap":"Direction matters: use components.","answer":"the vector sum of the forces due to each charge alone"},"phy-11-09-m09":{"idea":"Electric field is the force felt by one unit of positive charge.","steps":["Place a small positive test charge q at the point.","Measure the force F on it.","The field is E = F/q, in N/C, in the direction of the force."],"others":"Work from infinity is the potential. A force for a charge of either sign (magnitude only) loses the direction. Charge per area is surface charge density.","trap":"The test charge is small and positive.","answer":"the force per unit positive test charge placed at that point"},"phy-11-09-m10":{"idea":"A point charge produces a field that falls as 1/r² and points away from a positive charge.","steps":["From Coulomb's law with a unit test charge: E = Q/(4πε₀r²).","It falls with the square of the distance.","For positive Q the field points away from the charge."],"others":"Pointing towards a positive Q is wrong. Q r² grows with distance. 1/r is the wrong power.","trap":"Away from positive, towards negative.","answer":"E = Q/(4πε₀r²), directed away from Q if Q is positive"},"phy-11-09-m11":{"idea":"Field lines never cross, because the field at a point has only one direction.","steps":["At every point the field has one direction only.","If two lines crossed, there would be two directions at that point.","So field lines cannot cross.","The other three statements are true properties."],"others":"Lines do not form closed loops in electrostatics. They run from positive to negative charges. They are closer where the field is stronger.","trap":"The question asks which is NOT a property.","answer":"two field lines can cross each other at a point of the field"},"phy-11-09-m12":{"idea":"Flux through an area is the part of the field that goes straight through it.","steps":["Only the component of E along the normal passes through the area.","That component is E cos θ, with θ the angle to the normal.","So dφ = E dS cos θ."],"others":"sin θ would be right if θ were measured from the surface. E/dS and tan θ are not forms of the dot product.","trap":"θ is the angle between E and the normal, not the surface.","answer":"dφ = E dS cos θ"},"phy-11-09-m13":{"idea":"Gauss's law links the flux through a closed surface to the charge inside.","steps":["The total flux is φ = ∮E·dS = q/ε₀.","Only the charge enclosed counts.","The shape of the surface does not matter."],"others":"Charge outside gives no net flux. The flux is not always zero. q ε₀ multiplies instead of dividing.","trap":"Inside, divided by ε₀.","answer":"q/ε₀, where q is the total charge inside the surface"},"phy-11-09-m14":{"idea":"A cube has six identical faces, so each takes one-sixth of the flux.","steps":["The total flux from a charge q inside is q/ε₀.","By symmetry the six faces get equal shares.","Flux through one face = q/(6ε₀)."],"others":"6q/ε₀ multiplies instead of dividing. q/(4ε₀) is a share for four faces. q/ε₀ is the whole cube.","trap":"One face: total divided by six.","answer":"q/(6ε₀)"},"phy-11-09-m15":{"idea":"A charge outside a closed surface gives zero net flux.","steps":["Each field line that enters the surface must leave it again.","Flux in cancels flux out.","So the net flux is zero, even though the field at the surface is not zero."],"others":"Positive flux would need more lines leaving than entering. q/ε₀ is only for charge inside. Negative flux would need more lines entering than leaving.","trap":"Zero net flux does not mean zero field.","answer":"zero, as the flux that enters is equal to the flux that leaves"},"phy-11-09-m16":{"idea":"A dipole moment is charge times separation, pointing from the negative charge to the positive.","steps":["The separation of the charges is 2l.","The size is p = q × 2l.","The direction is from −q to +q."],"others":"p is not zero, because moment depends on separation, not on net charge. The direction from +q to −q is wrong. q/2l has the wrong dimensions.","trap":"Dipole moment points from negative to positive.","answer":"p = q × 2l, and it points from −q to +q"},"phy-11-09-m17":{"idea":"A dipole in a uniform field feels a turning force proportional to sin θ.","steps":["The two forces qE are equal and opposite, so the net force is zero.","They form a couple with arm 2l sin θ.","Torque = qE × 2l sin θ = pE sin θ."],"others":"cos θ would give zero torque at θ = 0°, which is wrong. The torque is zero along the field, so pE alone is wrong. p/(E sin θ) is not a torque formula.","trap":"Torque is zero when the dipole is along the field (θ = 0° or 180°).","answer":"τ = pE sin θ"},"phy-11-09-m18":{"idea":"In a uniform field a dipole does not move as a whole, but it can rotate.","steps":["The forces on +q and −q are equal and opposite, so the net force is zero.","They are not on the same line, so they make a torque.","The torque is zero only when the dipole is along the field."],"others":"The net force is not 2qE or qE. It is false that there is no torque, unless the dipole is along the field.","trap":"Zero force does not mean zero torque.","answer":"zero, but there is a torque on it unless it is along the field"},"phy-11-09-m19":{"idea":"On the equatorial line the sideways parts of the fields cancel, and what is left points against p.","steps":["On the perpendicular bisector the fields of +q and −q have equal size.","The parts along the bisector cancel.","The parts parallel to the dipole add up, pointing from + to −.","That is opposite to the dipole moment p."],"others":"The field is not perpendicular to p, and it is not along p. It is not zero, because the parallel parts add.","trap":"Axial field is along p. Equatorial field is opposite to p.","answer":"in the direction opposite to the dipole moment"},"phy-11-09-m20":{"idea":"A dipole field falls faster than a point-charge field, because the two charges nearly cancel.","steps":["On the axis of a short dipole: E = 2kp/r³.","A single charge gives E = kq/r².","So the dipole field falls as 1/r³, faster than 1/r²."],"others":"1/r⁴ is too fast. 1/r is slower than a single charge. 1/r² is the single charge result.","trap":"Point charge: 1/r². Dipole: 1/r³.","answer":"1/r³, faster than 1/r²"},"phy-11-09-m21":{"idea":"Charge densities say how charge is spread over a length, an area or a volume.","steps":["λ is charge per unit length (C/m).","σ is charge per unit area (C/m²).","ρ is charge per unit volume (C/m³)."],"others":"Charge per unit time is current. The other orderings swap length, area and volume.","trap":"Remember the order: λ line, σ surface, ρ volume.","answer":"charge per unit length, per unit area, and per unit volume"},"phy-11-09-m22":{"idea":"Inside a closed hollow conductor the field is zero. This is electrostatic shielding.","steps":["In electrostatics all excess charge sits on the outer surface.","By Gauss's law the field in an empty cavity is zero.","That is why a car is a safe place during lightning."],"others":"The field inside is not equal to the outside field. It is not proportional to distance. It is not uniform and non-zero.","trap":"Field inside a conductor is zero, even if the outside field is large.","answer":"zero, as the charge sits on the outer surface"},"phy-11-09-m23":{"idea":"Outside a charged spherical shell the field is the same as if all charge were at the centre.","steps":["Take a spherical Gaussian surface of radius r > R.","The flux is Q/ε₀ and by symmetry E × 4πr² = Q/ε₀.","So E = kQ/r², the same as for a point charge Q at the centre."],"others":"The field is not zero outside, only inside the shell. It is not half of the point charge field. It does not grow with r.","trap":"Inside a shell: zero. Outside: like a point charge.","answer":"the same as if all the charge Q were at the centre"},"phy-11-09-n01":{"idea":"Use Coulomb's law F = k q₁q₂/r².","steps":["q₁ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ C, q₂ = 3.0 × 10⁻⁶ C, r = 0.30 m.","q₁q₂ = 6.0 × 10⁻¹².","F = 9.0 × 10⁹ × 6.0 × 10⁻¹²/0.09 = 0.054/0.09.","F = 0.60 N (repulsive)."],"trap":"Convert μC to C first.","answer":"0.60 N"},"phy-11-09-n02":{"idea":"The field at a point is the vector sum of the fields of the two charges.","steps":["Distance from each charge: √(0.20² + 0.15²) = 0.25 m.","Each field: kq/r² = 9.0 × 10⁹ × 4.0 × 10⁻⁶/0.0625 = 5.76 × 10⁵ N/C.","The x-parts cancel. The y-parts add: 2 × 5.76 × 10⁵ × (0.15/0.25).","Total = 6.91 × 10⁵ N/C."],"trap":"By symmetry, only the component along the perpendicular bisector survives.","answer":"6.91 (in units of 10⁵ N/C)"},"phy-11-09-n03":{"idea":"Field of a point charge: E = kQ/r².","steps":["Q = 5.0 × 10⁻⁹ C, r = 0.30 m.","E = 9.0 × 10⁹ × 5.0 × 10⁻⁹/0.09.","E = 45/0.09 = 500 N/C."],"trap":"Check: Gauss's law gives the same result.","answer":"500 N/C"},"phy-11-09-n04":{"idea":"Gauss's law: total flux = q/ε₀.","steps":["q = 8.85 × 10⁻⁹ C and ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻¹² C²/(N m²).","φ = q/ε₀ = 8.85 × 10⁻⁹/8.85 × 10⁻¹².","φ = 1000 N m²/C."],"trap":"The shape of the closed surface does not matter.","answer":"1000 N m²/C"},"phy-11-09-n05":{"idea":"Torque on a dipole in a uniform field is pE sin θ.","steps":["p = q × 2l = 2.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 0.010 = 2.0 × 10⁻⁸ C m.","τ = pE sin θ = 2.0 × 10⁻⁸ × 1.0 × 10⁵ × sin 30°.","sin 30° = 0.5, so τ = 1.0 × 10⁻³ N m."],"trap":"Convert 1.0 cm to 0.010 m before multiplying.","answer":"1.0 (in units of 10⁻³ N m)"},"phy-11-09-n06":{"idea":"On the axis of a dipole, E = 2kpr/(r² − l²)².","steps":["p = 2.0 × 10⁻⁸ × 0.010 = 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁰ C m.","r = 0.50 m and l = 0.005 m, so l² is tiny compared with r².","E ≈ 2kp/r³ = 2 × 9.0 × 10⁹ × 2.0 × 10⁻¹⁰/0.125.","E = 3.6/0.125 = 28.8 N/C."],"trap":"For a short dipole (l much smaller than r) use the approximate 2kp/r³.","answer":"28.8 N/C"},"phy-11-09-n07":{"idea":"Total charge on a surface = surface charge density × area.","steps":["Area of a sphere = 4πR² = 4π × 0.10² = 0.1257 m².","q = σ × area = 1.0 × 10⁻⁶ × 0.1257.","q = 1.257 × 10⁻⁷ C."],"trap":"Use 4πR², not πR².","answer":"1.257 (in units of 10⁻⁷ C)"},"phy-11-09-n08":{"idea":"In a medium the force is the vacuum force divided by K.","steps":["Force in vacuum = 0.60 N (from k q₁q₂/r²).","K = 80 for water.","F = 0.60/80 = 0.0075 N."],"trap":"Water reduces electric forces strongly.","answer":"0.0075 N"},"phy-11-09-n09":{"idea":"A neutral point is where the two repulsive forces are equal and opposite.","steps":["Let the point be x from the 4.0 μC charge.","Equal forces: 4/x² = 1/(0.30 − x)².","Take square roots: 2/x = 1/(0.30 − x), so 2(0.30 − x) = x.","0.60 = 3x, so x = 0.200 m."],"trap":"The point lies closer to the smaller charge's side, so it is nearer the 1.0 μC charge: 0.10 m from it.","answer":"0.200 m"},"phy-11-09-n10":{"idea":"Add two equal forces at 60° to each other.","steps":["Each force: k q²/a² = 9.0 × 10⁹ × (2.0 × 10⁻⁶)²/0.01 = 3.6 N.","The two forces are 60° apart.","Net force = 2 × 3.6 × cos 30° = 7.2 × 0.866.","= 6.24 N."],"trap":"Use cos 30° (half the angle between the forces).","answer":"6.24 N"}};
